  "method": "1. Start your grain first. If it is white rice, simmer covered in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup of rice, about 18 minutes, then rest 5 minutes. If it is brown rice, simmer covered in 2 cups of water per cup of rice, 40 to 45 minutes, then rest 10 minutes. If it is quinoa, rinse well, then simmer covered in 2 cups of water per cup of quinoa, about 15 minutes, and rest 5 minutes. If it is the riced cauliflower, microwave it covered with a splash of water, 3 to 4 minutes, until hot. Weigh it cooked.\n2. Slice your vegetables into thin strips. If it is the tofu, press the tofu between towels under a heavy pan 10 minutes, so it crisps instead of steaming, then cut it into 3/4-inch cubes. If it is the TVP, soak the TVP in an equal amount of hot water 5 to 10 minutes, then squeeze it dry.\n3. Heat a little oil in a nonstick pan over medium-high until it shimmers. If it is the tofu, add the cubes in one layer and turn them every 2 minutes, 8 to 10 minutes, until golden and crisp. If it is the TVP, add the crumbles and stir 5 to 6 minutes, until browned and crisp. Sprinkle on 1/2 tsp chili powder, 1/4 tsp each cumin and smoked paprika, and a pinch of salt as it browns.\n4. Add your vegetables for the last 4 minutes, so they char and soften.\n5. Build the fajita bowl: the protein and vegetables over your grain with the salsa and the hot sauce. If it is the raw avocado, slice it and add it.",
  "oil": "Use avocado oil. Getting tofu genuinely crispy needs steady high heat, and refined avocado oil (smoke point ~520F) crisps the outside without smoking; olive oil would scorch before the tofu browns. The avocado on top is the raw, creamy fat, not cooked.",
  "season": "Optional: toss the tofu cubes with chili powder, cumin, smoked paprika, garlic powder, and a pinch of salt before searing. A squeeze of fresh lime at the end ties it all together.",
  "note": "Vegan fajita bowl: pan-crisped tofu with your charred vegetable over rice, finished with salsa and creamy avocado.",
