  "method": "1. Heat a little oil in a large skillet over medium-high and add your protein. If it is the ground turkey, let it sit 2 minutes before you break it up, so it browns instead of steaming. Cook 6 to 8 minutes, breaking it into small crumbles, until no pink remains and it reads 165F.\n2. Dice the zucchini small, stir it in and cook 3 to 4 minutes, until just tender.\n3. Stir in the marinara, lower the heat and simmer 8 minutes, until it thickens a little and the flavors come together.\n4. While the sauce simmers, cook your carb. If it is the cooked pasta, boil it in plenty of salted water, 8 to 11 minutes (6 to 8 for chickpea pasta, which goes from done to mushy fast), until just tender with a little bite, then drain it and weigh it cooked. If it is the Carbe Diem, weigh it dry, then boil it in plenty of salted water, 8 to 10 minutes, until tender with a little bite, and drain it. If it is the shirataki, tip the package into a sieve, rinse well under cold water, then dry-fry the noodles in a hot pan 2 to 3 minutes, until the water cooks off and the strands squeak.\n5. Toss your carb with the sauce, scatter the mozzarella over the top and cover the pan 1 minute, until it melts.\n6. Spoon it into a bowl. If your fat is the parmesan, sprinkle it on top. If your fat is the olive oil, drizzle the oil on top, off the heat, so its flavor stays fresh.",
  "oil": "Use a small amount of olive oil to start. Lean ground turkey or chicken renders very little fat of its own, so this bit of oil earns its keep. Keep the flame at medium-high, not blazing, so a regular olive oil stays under its smoke point and doesn't smoke; refined or light olive oil (~465F) gives you the most headroom for browning.",
