  "method": "1. Heat a little oil in a skillet over medium, then cook your vegetables, chopped, with 1 minced garlic clove 6 to 8 minutes, stirring, until soft and saucy, adding a splash of water if the pan looks dry.\n2. Drain and rinse your beans, because rinsing washes off the canning liquid.\n3. Stir your beans into the skillet with 1 tsp oregano, a pinch of chili flakes and black pepper, and simmer 5 to 8 minutes, until they turn creamy and the sauce thickens.\n4. If your carb is the pita, warm it in a dry pan 20 to 30 seconds a side. If it is the couscous, cook it: pour 1 cup of boiling water over each cup of couscous with a pinch of salt, cover and leave 5 minutes, then fluff it with a fork and weigh it cooked.\n5. Finish your beans with a squeeze of lemon and spoon them over your carb. If your fat is the olive oil, drizzle the olive oil over the top. If it is the goat cheese, tear the goat cheese into dollops and dot it over the hot beans, so it softens into them.",
  "oil": "Olive oil (about 375F smoke point) is ideal because the whole base cooks at gentle medium heat, well under its smoke point, and the same oil finishes the dish as a raw drizzle for grassy flavor. If you eat dairy, the soft goat cheese is the second fat, it melts into the warm beans for tang and creaminess with no extra cooking.",
  "season": "Oregano, black pepper, a pinch of chili flakes, and a squeeze of lemon at the end. A bay leaf simmered in and removed deepens it. Optional garnish: torn fresh basil or parsley. Salt to taste (goat cheese, if used, adds some), garlic",
