  "method": "1. If your grain is the brown rice or the white rice, simmer your grain covered in 2 cups of water per cup of rice, 40 to 45 minutes for brown or 16 to 18 minutes for white, then rest 5 minutes and weigh it cooked. If it is the quinoa, rinse it and simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup 15 minutes, then rest 5 minutes and weigh it cooked. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook it in a dry skillet 4 to 5 minutes, until tender.\n2. Season your protein with 1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp oregano, 1 tsp smoked paprika, 1/2 tsp garlic powder and salt, and cut whole cuts into bite-size chunks.\n3. Heat a little oil in a skillet over medium-high and cook your protein 6 to 8 minutes, turning or stirring, until deeply browned with crispy carnitas-style edges (145F for whole pork, 160F for ground pork), because a hard sear is where the carnitas flavor comes from.\n4. Slice your vegetables and add the slices to the pan for the last 3 to 4 minutes, until softened and lightly charred at the edges.\n5. Drain the pinto beans in a colander, rinsing them under cold water. Warm the pinto beans in a small pot with a splash of water and 1/2 tsp cumin, 3 to 4 minutes, mashing a few until creamy.\n6. Spoon your grain and the beans into a bowl, top with your protein and the vegetables, then add the salsa, the cheddar, sliced avocado and a squeeze of lime.",
  "oil": "Avocado oil (about 520F smoke point), real carnitas texture comes from a hard, high-heat sear, and avocado oil takes that heat without smoking. Lean pork renders almost no fat of its own, so a teaspoon keeps it from sticking and helps a crisp crust form. The avocado fruit on top is the cool, creamy finishing fat.",
