  "method": "1. If your grain is the white rice, rinse it (jasmine is the classic pick), then simmer it covered in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup, 18 minutes, until the water is gone. If it is the brown rice, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup, 40 to 45 minutes, until tender. If it is the quinoa, rinse it, then simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup, 15 minutes, until the water is gone. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook your grain in a dry skillet, 3 to 4 minutes, until tender and dry. Weigh your grain cooked.\n2. Pat the shrimp dry and salt them lightly, because a dry surface sears instead of steaming.\n3. Heat a little oil in a wok or wide skillet over high heat until it shimmers, then sear the shrimp in one layer, 1 to 2 minutes a side, until pink and opaque (145F). Move them to a plate, so they do not turn rubbery.\n4. If your vegetables are the bok choy, halve it lengthwise and sear it cut side down in the same pan 2 to 3 minutes, until crisp-tender. If it is the carrots, slice them thin and stir-fry them 4 to 5 minutes. If it is the snap peas, stir-fry them 2 to 3 minutes, until bright green.\n5. Stir in 2 minced garlic cloves and 1 tsp grated ginger for 30 seconds, then add the soy sauce, sriracha and a splash of water.\n6. Return the shrimp and toss 30 seconds to coat. Serve over your grain with a squeeze of lime, a sprinkle of sesame seeds and sliced scallion.",
  "oil": "Avocado oil (about 520F smoke point) for the screaming-hot wok, shrimp and the vegetable both want a fast, high-heat sear and avocado oil holds up without smoking; canola (450F) is the neutral budget alternative. Skip olive and flax here, since they scorch and turn bitter at stir-fry heat.",
  "season": "A mountain of garlic and fresh ginger, this dish lives on them, plus a few drops of sriracha and a squeeze of lime at the end. Optional garnish: toasted sesame seeds and sliced scallion. For an authentic finish, a few drops of toasted sesame oil off the heat.",
