  "method": "1. If your grain is the white rice, rinse it, then simmer it covered in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup, 18 minutes, until the water is gone. If it is the brown rice, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup, 40 to 45 minutes, until tender. If it is the quinoa, rinse it, then simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup, 15 minutes, until the water is gone. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook your grain in a dry skillet, 3 to 4 minutes, until tender and dry. Weigh your grain cooked.\n2. Pat the chicken dry, so it browns. If your protein is the chicken breast, cut it into bite-size pieces. If it is the chicken thigh, cut it into bite-size pieces. If it is the leg quarters, cook them in a covered skillet over medium-low first, 35 to 45 minutes, turning once, until 165F near the bone, then pull the meat off the bone in chunks and leave the skin behind.\n3. If your fat is the cashews, toast them in a dry wok 2 to 3 minutes, until golden, and set them aside.\n4. Heat a little oil in a wok or large skillet over high heat, then sear the chicken in one layer, 5 to 6 minutes, stirring now and then, until browned and 165F inside.\n5. Add your vegetables and stir-fry 3 to 4 minutes, until crisp-tender. If your vegetables include the broccoli, add a splash of water and cover 1 minute so it steams.\n6. Add a splash of soy sauce, 1 tsp honey, the sriracha, 2 minced garlic cloves and 1 tsp ginger, and toss 1 minute to glaze.\n7. If your fat is the cashews, fold them in last, so they stay crunchy. Pile the stir-fry over your grain.",
  "oil": "A little avocado oil (smoke point ~520F) for the ripping-hot wok sear, high, neutral, and built for stir-frying without smoking. If your fat is cashews, those are the real richness here: toast them dry first and add them last so they don't go soft. Skip lower-heat oils like olive or butter; they'd burn at this heat.",
