  "method": "1. If your grain is the white rice, rinse it (basmati is the classic pick), then simmer it covered in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup, 18 minutes, until the water is gone. If it is the brown rice, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup, 40 to 45 minutes, until tender. If it is the quinoa, rinse it, then simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup, 15 minutes, until the water is gone. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook your grain in a dry skillet, 3 to 4 minutes, until tender and dry. Weigh your grain cooked.\n2. Drain and rinse the chickpeas, which washes off the canning salt.\n3. Heat your fat in a deep pan over medium heat. If your vegetables include the onion, dice it fine and cook it 6 to 8 minutes, stirring often, until deep golden, because that color is the base of the dish.\n4. Stir in 2 grated garlic cloves, 1 tsp grated ginger, 1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp coriander, 1/2 tsp turmeric, 1 tsp garam masala and a pinch of chili flakes for 30 seconds, until fragrant. If your vegetables include the tomato, chop it and cook it 4 to 5 minutes, until it breaks down into a jammy paste.\n5. Add the chickpeas and a splash of water, mash a handful against the side of the pan, and simmer 10 to 12 minutes, until the sauce is thick and clings.\n6. Serve over your grain with a squeeze of lemon and chopped cilantro, then spoon the Greek yogurt on top.",
  "oil": "Coconut oil (refined, smoke point ~350F) over medium heat suits this slow onion-and-tomato base and adds an authentic richness. Avocado oil (~520F) is a clean neutral swap if you want zero coconut flavor. Either way there's no hard sear here, so medium heat is all you need, keep it moderate so nothing scorches.",
  "season": "Salt the onion base as it softens. Cumin, coriander, turmeric, garam masala, a pinch of chili, and fresh ginger bloomed in with the garlic. A squeeze of lemon and chopped cilantro at the end is the classic bright finish and well worth it. If you eat dairy, a spoon of yogurt on top cools the spice.",
  "note": "A North-Indian chickpea curry in a jammy onion-tomato gravy over rice. Naturally vegan and built entirely from pantry staples.",
