  "method": "1. If your protein is the split peas, rinse them, then simmer them in 3 cups of water per cup, 25 to 40 minutes, until soft. If it is the lentils, rinse them, then simmer them in 3 cups of water per cup, 18 to 25 minutes, until soft. Weigh your protein cooked.\n2. If your grain is the white rice, rinse it, then simmer it covered in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup, 18 minutes, until the water is gone. If it is the brown rice, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup, 40 to 45 minutes, until tender. If it is the quinoa, rinse it, then simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup, 15 minutes, until the water is gone. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook your grain in a dry skillet, 3 to 4 minutes, until tender and dry. Weigh your grain cooked.\n3. If your vegetables include the tomato, dice it and stir it into the dal for the last 5 minutes. Whisk or mash the dal with 1/2 tsp turmeric until soupy, adding water a splash at a time.\n4. Make the tadka: heat your fat (butter is traditional) in a small pan over medium-low. If your vegetables include the onion, slice it thin and fry it 4 to 5 minutes, until golden. Add 1 tsp cumin seeds and 2 sliced garlic cloves and fry 1 to 2 minutes, until fragrant, because garlic burns fast.\n5. Pour the sizzling tadka over the dal and stir it through. Serve over your grain with a squeeze of lemon and cilantro, then spoon the Greek yogurt on top.",
  "oil": "Salted butter over medium-low heat (browns ~300F, burns fast) is the traditional tadka fat and carries the toasted-cumin flavor, keep the heat gentle so the milk solids turn golden without scorching. Coconut oil (~350F) is the vegan swap and handles the same low heat just fine. Neither is a high-heat oil, so never crank the burner here.",
