  "prefProduce": "Spinach cooked",
  "diets": [
   "omnivore",
   "pescatarian",
   "vegetarian"
  ],
  "method": "1. If your grain is the white rice, rinse it, then simmer it covered in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup, 18 minutes, until the water is gone, and weigh it cooked. If it is the brown rice, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup, 40 to 45 minutes, until tender, and weigh it cooked. If it is the quinoa, rinse it, then simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup, 15 minutes, until the water is gone, and weigh it cooked. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook your grain in a dry skillet, 3 to 4 minutes, until tender and dry, and weigh it cooked.\n2. Heat your fat in a wide pan over medium heat and stir in 2 grated garlic cloves, 1 tsp grated ginger, 1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp coriander, 1/2 tsp turmeric, 1 tsp garam masala and a pinch of chili flakes. If your vegetables include the tomato, chop it, add it and cook 3 to 4 minutes, until it softens.\n3. If your vegetables include the spinach, add it and stir 3 to 4 minutes, until fully wilted and silky, then mash it roughly into a coarse sauce. Loosen with a splash of water or milk.\n4. Take the pan off the heat and fold in your protein (ricotta or cottage cheese) in spoonfuls, so it stays in soft creamy curds in place of paneer and does not go grainy.\n5. Serve with your grain and a squeeze of lemon, then spoon the Greek yogurt on top. If your carb is the pita, warm it in a dry pan 30 seconds a side, to scoop with.",
  "oil": "Salted butter over medium heat (browns ~300F) gives this its rich restaurant flavor, keep the heat moderate so it never smokes. Coconut oil (~350F) is a fine swap if you prefer. Either way the cheese (ricotta or cottage cheese) is the protein and creaminess, not a cooking fat, and goes in off the heat.",
  "season": "Optional: garam masala, cumin, coriander, turmeric, and a pinch of chili. A little grated ginger and a squeeze of lemon at the end cut the richness; a swirl of yogurt on top is a nice touch.",
  "note": "A lighter palak paneer using soft cheese curds in place of paneer, folded into a spiced creamy spinach sauce over rice. Vegetarian (contains dairy).",
