  "method": "1. Heat the oven to 400F. Scrub the sweet potato, prick it with a fork and bake it on the rack 45 to 55 minutes, until a knife slides in with no resistance. Start it first, because it takes the longest.\n2. Trim and halve the Brussels sprouts, toss with a little oil and salt, and spread them cut side down on a sheet pan. Roast 20 to 25 minutes, until deeply browned and crisp at the edges.\n3. Pat the pork tenderloin dry and season it with salt, pepper and a pinch of smoked paprika, because wet meat will not brown. Stir the mustard and maple syrup together with 1/2 tsp minced garlic for the glaze.\n4. Heat a thin film of oil in an oven-safe skillet over medium-high until it shimmers, then sear the pork 2 to 3 minutes a side, turning to brown all sides, 8 to 10 minutes in all.\n5. Brush the glaze over the pork and roast it 12 to 15 minutes, until a thermometer in the thickest part reads 145F.\n6. Rest the pork 5 minutes, so the juices stay in, then slice it into medallions. Plate it with the Brussels sprouts and the split sweet potato.",
  "oil": "Use avocado oil. The hard stovetop sear needs a near-smoking pan, and refined avocado oil's ~520F smoke point handles that without burning, then carries through the 400F roast of the Brussels too. Olive oil or butter would scorch on a real sear, so avocado is the right pick here.",
  "season": "Optional glaze: yellow or Dijon mustard stirred with maple syrup and a little minced garlic. Season the pork with salt, pepper, and a pinch of smoked paprika. Finish the Brussels with cracked black pepper and a squeeze of lemon to balance their richness.",
