  "method": "1. Scrub the mussels under cold water and pull off any stringy beards. Tap any open mussel on the counter and throw away the ones that do not slowly close, because they are dead.\n2. Slice your vegetables thin. If your produce is the leeks, cut them into half moons and rinse them well, because grit hides between the layers.\n3. Warm your fat in a wide pot over medium heat, add the vegetables and soften them 4 to 5 minutes, until limp, adding 3 cloves minced garlic for the last minute.\n4. Pour in 1/2 cup of broth or water and bring it to a simmer.\n5. Toast your carb 2 to 3 minutes, until crisp and golden, so it is sturdy enough to mop up the broth.\n6. Tip in the mussels, cover the pot and steam 5 to 7 minutes, shaking the pot once, until the shells open. Throw away any that stay shut.\n7. Pile the mussels into bowls, ladle the broth over them, add a squeeze of lemon, 2 tbsp chopped parsley and the hot sauce, and serve with the toast for dipping.",
  "oil": "Butter over medium heat builds the classic mariniere flavor in the broth; keep the heat moderate, since butter browns past ~350F. Olive oil (the other listed fat) works just as well if you'd rather skip the dairy. Either way the heat stays gentle, so a high-smoke-point oil isn't needed here.",
  "season": "Optional: lots of garlic, fresh parsley, a bay leaf, cracked pepper, and a squeeze of lemon. The mussels release their own briny liquor as they open, so go easy on added salt and taste the broth before adjusting.",
