  "prefProtein": "chicken breast cooked",
  "prefProduce": "romaine lettuce",
  "diets": [
   "omnivore"
  ],
  "method": "1. If your protein is the chicken leg quarters, season them with a pinch of salt and pepper and 1 tsp paprika and roast at 425F 40 to 45 minutes, until golden and 165F at the thickest part, then rest them 5 minutes, pull the chicken off the bone and slice it.\n2. Halve the baby potatoes, cover them with salted water and simmer 12 to 15 minutes, until a fork slides in easily, then drain them.\n3. If your protein is the chicken breast, season it with a pinch of salt and pepper, sear it in a little oil over medium-high heat 5 to 6 minutes a side, until golden and 165F in the thickest part, then rest it 5 minutes and slice it. If it is the egg, lower it into boiling water and cook 10 to 12 minutes, until the yolk is firm all the way through (160F), then cool it in cold water, peel it and halve it. If it is the bacon, lay it in a cold pan and cook over medium heat 4 to 5 minutes a side, until crisp, then drain it and crumble it.\n4. Chop your vegetables and slice the avocado.\n5. Whisk 1 tbsp lemon juice, 1 tsp red wine vinegar, 1 tsp mustard, a pinch of salt and pepper and a splash of water into a quick dressing.\n6. Build the cobb in tidy rows over the vegetables with your protein, the potatoes, the cheddar and the avocado, then drizzle with the dressing just before eating, because it keeps the lettuce crisp.",
  "oil": "If you are searing chicken, use a thin coat of avocado oil (smoke point ~520F), it handles a hard sear without breaking down; a quick mist of avocado-oil spray works on a tight fat budget. Reserve extra-virgin olive oil (smoke point ~375F) for the dressing only, used raw and unheated, its smoke point doesn't matter and you want its fruity, peppery flavor. Do not sear in olive oil; it would smoke. (Egg and bacon don't need added oil.)",
  "season": "Optional: salt, cracked black pepper, and a little garlic powder on the chicken. A squeeze of lemon over the avocado keeps it green. A small spoon of Dijon-style yellow mustard whisked into the dressing makes it taste like a steakhouse cobb.",
