  "method": "1. Start your grain first. Weigh it cooked. If your grain is the bulgur, pour 1 1/2 cups of boiling water per cup of bulgur over it, cover and steep 12 to 15 minutes, then fluff it. If it is the couscous, pour 1 cup of boiling water per cup of couscous over it, cover and steep 5 minutes, then fluff it. If it is the quinoa, rinse it and simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of quinoa 15 minutes, then fluff it.\n2. Heat a little oil in a skillet over medium heat. If your protein is the falafel, pan-fry it 2 to 3 minutes a side, until crunchy outside and hot through. If it is the chickpeas, drain and rinse them, pat them dry and crisp them 5 to 6 minutes, shaking the pan often, until golden.\n3. Dice, slice or tear your vegetables.\n4. If your fat is the tahini, stir it with the juice of 1/2 lemon, 1 grated garlic clove, a pinch of salt and a splash of water until pourable, adding the water slowly because it seizes before it loosens. If it is the olive oil, whisk it with the juice of 1/2 lemon, 1 grated garlic clove and a pinch of salt.\n5. Build the bowl with your grain on the bottom, then your protein, your vegetables, the hummus and the pickles, and drizzle the sauce over the top.",
  "oil": "Tahini is the finishing fat and the whole flavor engine here, thinned with lemon and water into a creamy sauce, no cooking oil required. If you pan-crisp the falafel or chickpeas rather than bake them, a thin film of avocado oil (smoke point ~520F) handles medium-high heat without burning; honest medium-heat olive oil is fine too.",
