  "method": "1. Cook the lentils: rinse them and simmer them in 3 cups of water per cup of lentils, 18 to 25 minutes, until tender but still holding their shape, then drain them (canned lentils only need draining and rinsing). Weigh them cooked, and serve them warm or at room temperature.\n2. If your fat is the walnuts, toast them in a dry pan over medium heat 2 to 3 minutes, shaking often, until they smell nutty, and watch them closely because they burn fast.\n3. If your produce is the beets, dice them into bite size pieces. If it is the onion, slice it paper thin.\n4. Whisk 1 tbsp lemon juice or balsamic vinegar with 1 tsp mustard, a pinch of salt and pepper and a splash of water. If your fat is the olive oil, whisk it in too.\n5. Pile your vegetables on a plate, spoon the lentils over them and drizzle with the dressing. If your protein is the goat cheese, crumble it over the lentils. If your fat is the walnuts, scatter them over the top. If it is the goat cheese, crumble it over the top.",
  "oil": "Extra-virgin olive oil (smoke point ~375F) whisked raw into the vinaigrette, no heat, so its lower smoke point doesn't matter and the fruity flavor shines against the earthy beets and lentils. The walnuts are dry-toasted with no oil, and a little soft goat cheese adds creaminess if you eat dairy. Nothing needs a hot pan here.",
  "season": "Optional: a little Dijon-style mustard and a splash of balsamic in the dressing, plus salt, cracked pepper, and fresh thyme or parsley. A pinch of lemon or orange zest over the beets lifts everything.",
  "note": "A French bistro salad, earthy lentils and sweet beets over arugula with toasted walnuts (goat cheese optional if you eat dairy).",
