  "method": "1. Start your grain first. Weigh it cooked. If your grain is the white rice, simmer it covered in 1 1/2 cups water per cup of rice 18 minutes and rest it 5 minutes. If it is the brown rice, simmer it covered in 2 cups water per cup of rice 40 to 45 minutes. If it is the quinoa, rinse it and simmer it covered in 2 cups water per cup of quinoa 15 minutes. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook it covered in a nonstick pan over medium heat 4 to 5 minutes, until tender.\n2. Heat a little oil in a wide pan over medium-high heat. If your protein is the tofu, press it 10 minutes, cube it and sear it 8 to 10 minutes, turning, until golden on several sides. If it is the TVP, soak it in hot water 10 minutes, squeeze it dry and brown it 5 to 6 minutes, until crisp at the edges.\n3. Cut your vegetables small, add them to the pan and cook 6 to 8 minutes, until soft and browned at the edges.\n4. Whisk the miso (a gluten-free one if you avoid gluten) with 1 tsp maple syrup, 1 tbsp tamari and 2 tbsp water until smooth. Lower the heat to medium, pour the glaze over the pan and toss 1 to 2 minutes, until glossy, keeping it moving because miso scorches fast.\n5. Spoon your grain into a bowl donburi style, top with the glazed protein and vegetables and add the hot sauce. If your fat is the tahini, drizzle it over the top.",
  "oil": "Sear in avocado oil (smoke point ~520F): it takes the medium-high heat that collapses and caramelizes the eggplant and crisps the tofu without smoking. Drop the heat to medium before the certified gluten-free miso glaze goes in, because certified gluten-free miso burns fast. If your plan's fat is tahini, thread a little raw tahini over the finished bowl, it adds richness with no cooking.",
