  "method": "1. Cook your grain. If it is the buckwheat groats, rinse them and simmer them covered in 2 cups of water per cup of groats, 10 to 15 minutes, until tender. If it is the brown rice, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of rice, 40 to 45 minutes, until tender. If it is the quinoa, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of quinoa, 12 to 15 minutes, until fluffy. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook it covered in a hot pan with a splash of water, 4 to 5 minutes, until tender. Weigh it cooked.\n2. Cook your protein. If it is the edamame, simmer it in a pot of water 3 to 5 minutes, until bright green and tender, then drain it. If it is the soybeans, drain and rinse them.\n3. Make the sauce: whisk 1 tbsp tamari, 1 tsp rice vinegar, 1 tsp grated ginger, a splash of water and your fat in a small bowl, until smooth and pourable.\n4. Slice your vegetables thin, so they cook in minutes. Heat a little oil in a wide pan or wok over high heat and stir-fry them 3 to 4 minutes, until crisp-tender.\n5. Add your grain and your protein to the pan and toss 1 minute, until hot.\n6. Pour in the sauce and toss to coat, then serve in a bowl topped with the sriracha and a sprinkle of sesame seeds.",
  "oil": "Two fats, two jobs. Stir-fry in avocado oil (smoke point ~520F) because the pan runs hot and olive oil or butter would smoke. If your plan's fat is tahini instead, whisk it raw into the finishing sauce (do not heat it, it is a dressing) and use just a light mist of avocado oil to stir-fry.",
