  "season": "Optional: soy sauce (or tamari) and white vinegar in equal measure (the adobo backbone), lots of garlic, a bay leaf, whole black peppercorns, and sliced scallion. Fish sauce is optional depth.",
  "note": "Adobo is the Filipino national dish: chicken braised low and slow in tamari (gluten-free soy sauce), vinegar, garlic, bay, and peppercorns until the sauce turns deep and glossy. Honey rounds the tang without making it sweet.",
  "method": "1. Cook your grain. If it is the white rice, simmer it covered in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup of rice, 15 to 18 minutes, until tender. If it is the brown rice, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of rice, 40 to 45 minutes, until tender. If it is the quinoa, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of quinoa, 12 to 15 minutes, until fluffy. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook it covered in a hot pan with a splash of water, 4 to 5 minutes, until tender. Weigh it cooked.\n2. Heat your fat in a wide pot over medium heat and brown your protein 2 minutes a side, then set it aside.\n3. If it is the onion, slice it and cook it in the pot 2 to 3 minutes, until soft. Add 6 minced garlic cloves and cook 30 seconds, until fragrant.\n4. Add the tamari, 1/4 cup cider vinegar, 1/4 cup water, 1/2 tsp black pepper and the honey, then return your protein. Bring to a simmer and let it bubble 2 minutes before stirring, so the sharp vinegar edge cooks off.\n5. If it is the chicken breast, simmer it covered on low 12 to 15 minutes, until 165F. If it is the chicken thigh, simmer it covered on low 20 to 25 minutes, until 165F. If it is the chicken drumstick, simmer it covered on low 25 to 30 minutes, until 165F. If it is the chicken leg quarters, simmer it covered on low 35 to 40 minutes, until 165F. Let the sauce bubble uncovered 2 to 3 minutes, until it glazes your protein.\n6. If it is the green beans, boil them 4 to 5 minutes, until just tender. If it is the bok choy, steam it 2 to 3 minutes, until just tender.\n7. Spoon your grain onto a plate, lay the chicken on top and ladle over the sauce. Set your vegetables alongside and top with sliced scallion.",
