  "season": "garlic, onion, fish sauce, bay leaf, black pepper, fresh tomato or tomato paste, sliced green onion",
  "note": "Giniling is the everyday Filipino ground-meat stew, simmered with potato, carrot, and peas in a savory tomato-tamari (gluten-free soy sauce) base. Lean ground and an egg-fried-rice-free plain rice keep it macro friendly.",
  "method": "1. Cook your grain. If it is the white rice, simmer it covered in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup of rice, 15 to 18 minutes, until tender. If it is the brown rice, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of rice, 40 to 45 minutes, until tender. If it is the quinoa, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of quinoa, 12 to 15 minutes, until fluffy. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook it covered in a hot pan with a splash of water, 4 to 5 minutes, until tender. Weigh it cooked.\n2. Heat your fat in a wide pan over medium heat and cook 3 minced garlic cloves and 1/2 cup diced onion 2 to 3 minutes, until soft.\n3. If it is the ground pork, add it, break it up and brown it over medium-high heat 5 to 6 minutes, until no pink remains (160F). If it is the ground beef, add it, break it up and brown it over medium-high heat 5 to 6 minutes, until no pink remains (160F). If it is the ground turkey, add it, break it up and brown it over medium-high heat 5 to 6 minutes, until no pink remains (165F).\n4. Stir in the tamari, 1 tsp fish sauce, the ketchup and plenty of black pepper, with a splash of water, so the sauce coats the meat. Bring to a simmer.\n5. If it is the potato, dice it small and simmer it in the pan, covered, 10 to 12 minutes, until fork tender. If it is the carrots, dice them small and simmer them in the pan, covered, 8 to 10 minutes, until tender. If it is the green peas, stir them in and simmer 2 minutes, until hot.\n6. Spoon your grain onto a plate and ladle the giniling and its sauce over it. Top with sliced scallion.",
