  "season": "Dashi (the soul of oyakodon), tamari, mirin or a little honey, and sliced scallion over the top. A few slices of pickled ginger on the side cut through the sweetness. A shake of shichimi togarashi at the table.",
  "note": "Oyakodon means parent-and-child bowl, named for the chicken and egg together. It is naturally lean and high in protein when built on chicken breast or thigh.",
  "method": "1. Start your grain. If it is the white rice, rinse it, simmer it covered in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup of rice for about 18 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the brown rice, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of rice for about 40 minutes, rest it 10 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the quinoa, rinse it, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of quinoa for about 15 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook it in a hot skillet for 3 to 5 minutes, stirring, until tender, then weigh it cooked.\n2. Slice the onion into thin half-moons, then cut your protein into bite-size pieces, off the bone if it has one.\n3. Beat the eggs with a fork just until the yolks break, leaving a few streaks of white.\n4. In a shallow pan, stir the soy sauce, coconut aminos and honey with about 1/3 cup of water and bring it to a gentle simmer over medium heat.\n5. Add the onion and simmer 2 minutes until soft, then add your protein and simmer 5 to 7 minutes, until no longer pink inside (165F).\n6. Pour the eggs over the top in a thin stream and do not stir, so they set in soft layers. Cover and cook 1 to 2 minutes, until set (160F).\n7. Spoon your grain into a bowl, slide the onion, the eggs and your protein on top and spoon a little of the broth over it.",
