  "season": "Fresh ginger and garlic, tamari, a splash of rice vinegar, and a gluten-free hoisin-style sauce (the moo shu signature), with a few drops of toasted sesame oil. Sliced scallion on top.",
  "note": "A lighter take on moo shu pork, served over rice instead of fried pancakes. Lean pork tenderloin and egg give two protein sources while the cabbage and snap peas keep it low-calorie.",
  "method": "1. Start your grain. If it is the white rice, rinse it, simmer it covered in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup of rice for about 18 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the brown rice, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of rice for about 40 minutes, rest it 10 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the quinoa, rinse it, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of quinoa for about 15 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook it in a hot skillet for 3 to 5 minutes, stirring, until tender, then weigh it cooked.\n2. Slice your protein into thin strips, off the bone if it has one, and slice your vegetables thin, so everything cooks in a few minutes. If it is the snap peas, trim the ends and leave them whole.\n3. Beat the eggs. Heat a little oil in a wide pan over medium-high, pour in the eggs and stir 1 to 2 minutes, until set with no runny parts (160F), then scoop them onto a plate.\n4. Add a little more oil, turn the heat to high and stir-fry your protein. If it is the pork tenderloin, cook it 3 to 4 minutes, until no pink shows (145F). If it is the chicken breast, cook it 4 to 5 minutes, until no pink shows (165F). If it is the chicken leg quarter, cook it 4 to 5 minutes, until no pink shows (165F).\n5. Add your vegetables and stir-fry 3 to 4 minutes, until just tender but still crisp.\n6. Stir in the soy sauce, coconut aminos and grated garlic, toss 1 minute, then fold the eggs back in.\n7. Spoon your grain into a bowl and pile the stir-fry on top. Finish with sliced scallion and a splash of rice vinegar.",
