  "season": "onion, paprika, cumin, cayenne, sumac, parsley, salt, black pepper",
  "note": "Adana kebab is normally grilled on skewers. The grated onion keeps the meat juicy. The sumac-onion salad cuts the richness and is not optional for the real flavor.",
  "method": "1. Start your grain. If it is the bulgur, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of bulgur for about 12 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the white rice, rinse it, simmer it covered in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup of rice for about 18 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the quinoa, rinse it, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of quinoa for about 15 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook it in a hot skillet for 3 to 5 minutes, stirring, until tender, then weigh it cooked.\n2. Mix your protein with 1 tsp paprika, 1/2 tsp cumin, a pinch of cayenne, 1/2 tsp salt and plenty of pepper. Knead it 2 minutes, until sticky, so it holds together on the pan.\n3. Wet your hands and press it into 3 or 4 long flat ovals, like fat fingers, so they cook evenly.\n4. Heat the olive oil in a pan over medium-high. If your protein is the ground lamb, cook the kofte 4 to 5 minutes a side, until browned and no pink remains (160F). If your protein is the ground beef, cook the kofte 4 to 5 minutes a side, until browned and no pink remains (160F). If your protein is the ground turkey, cook the kofte 4 to 5 minutes a side, until browned and no pink remains (165F).\n5. Meanwhile, slice your vegetables thin and toss them with a squeeze of lemon, chopped parsley and a pinch of salt for a quick salad, so it cuts the richness.\n6. Spoon your grain onto a plate, lay the kofte on top, add the salad on the side and spoon on the Greek yogurt.",
