 "fat": "Olive oil",
 "produce": "Tomato raw|Onion raw",
 "season": "onion, garlic, tomato paste, allspice, cumin, parsley, lemon juice, salt, black pepper",
 "note": "Fasolia is the cozy Levantine bean stew, warm with allspice and tomato. White beans and lean lamb make it filling and very high protein. Always served over plain rice.",
 "method": "1. Start your grain. If it is the white rice, rinse it, simmer it covered in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup of rice for about 18 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the brown rice, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of rice for about 40 minutes, rest it 10 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the quinoa, rinse it, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of quinoa for about 15 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook it in a hot skillet for 3 to 5 minutes, stirring, until tender, then weigh it cooked.\n2. Cut your protein into bite-size cubes, off the bone if it has one, and season it with salt and pepper.\n3. Heat the olive oil in a pot over medium-high and brown your protein on all sides, about 5 minutes. Scoop it out and leave the browned bits in the pot, because they flavor the stew.\n4. Lower the heat to medium. Chop your vegetables fine and cook them 5 minutes, until soft, then stir in 2 cloves minced garlic and cook 1 more minute.\n5. Stir in 2 tbsp tomato paste, 1/2 tsp cumin, 1/4 tsp cinnamon, salt and pepper and cook 1 minute, so the paste loses its raw taste.\n6. Drain and rinse the white beans. Return your protein to the pot with the beans and about a cup of water, cover and simmer gently 20 to 25 minutes, until it is tender and cooked through (145F).\n7. Stir in a handful of chopped parsley. Spoon your grain onto a plate, ladle the stew over it and finish with a squeeze of lemon.",
