  "note": "Muhammara is the Syrian roasted red pepper and walnut dip. Turned into a bowl with lean chicken and bulgur it stays high protein while keeping the smoky, sweet-tart pepper flavor.",
  "method": "1. Start your grain. If it is the bulgur, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of bulgur for about 12 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the couscous, pour an equal amount of boiling water over it with a pinch of salt, cover and let it stand 5 minutes, fluff it with a fork, then weigh it cooked.\n2. Roast your vegetables under a hot broiler or in a hot dry pan, turning, until the skins blacken and blister. If it is the red bell pepper, roast it whole for 8 to 10 minutes. If it is the tomato, halve it and roast it 5 to 6 minutes.\n3. Let them steam in a covered bowl 5 minutes, then peel and chop them, so the skins slip off.\n4. Chop the walnuts and mash them with the roasted vegetables, a squeeze of lemon, 1/2 tsp cumin, a pinch of cayenne, a pinch of salt and a drizzle of the olive oil into a thick, rough muhammara.\n5. Cut your protein into thin strips, off the bone if it has one, and toss it with 1 tsp paprika and a pinch of salt and pepper.\n6. Heat the olive oil in a pan over medium-high and cook your protein 5 to 7 minutes, stirring, until browned and cooked through (165F).\n7. Stir chopped parsley into your grain. Spoon it into a bowl, lay your protein on top, add a big dollop of the muhammara on the side and scatter the pomegranate seeds over the top.",
