  "season": "berbere spice, garlic, ginger, ground cardamom, a pinch of fenugreek, salt, black pepper",
  "note": "Harissa stands in for berbere; for a more authentic flavor add a pinch each of ground fenugreek and ground clove with the ginger in step 1.",
  "method": "1. Start your grain. If it is the white rice, rinse it, simmer it covered in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup of rice for about 18 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the brown rice, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of rice for about 40 minutes, rest it 10 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the quinoa, rinse it, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of quinoa for about 15 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook it in a hot skillet for 3 to 5 minutes, stirring, until tender, then weigh it cooked.\n2. Pat your protein dry and season it all over with salt, pepper and 1/2 tsp ground ginger.\n3. Chop the onion fine. Heat a little oil in a deep pan over medium, add the onion and cook slowly 8 minutes, stirring often, until very soft and golden, because this slow onion is the base of the sauce.\n4. Stir in the garlic and the harissa, which stands in for berbere here, and cook 1 minute, so the spice loses its raw smell. Add a splash of water for a thick, glossy sauce.\n5. Add your protein and turn it in the sauce, then pour in water to come halfway up and cover. If it is the chicken breast, simmer it gently 15 to 18 minutes, until cooked through (165F). If it is the chicken thigh, simmer it gently 20 to 25 minutes, until cooked through (165F). If it is the chicken drumstick, simmer it gently 30 to 35 minutes, until cooked through (165F). If it is the chicken leg quarter, simmer it gently 35 to 40 minutes, until cooked through (165F).\n6. Meanwhile, rinse and chop your greens. If it is the collard greens, simmer them in a splash of water with a pinch of salt 10 to 12 minutes, until tender, then squeeze them dry. If it is the kale, simmer it in a splash of water with a pinch of salt 6 to 8 minutes, until tender, then squeeze it dry. If it is the spinach, stir it in a hot pan with a splash of water 1 to 2 minutes, until wilted, then squeeze it dry.\n7. Taste the sauce and add salt if it needs it. Spoon your grain onto a plate, pile your greens beside it and top with your protein and its sauce.",
