  "method": "1. Start your grain. If it is the white rice, rinse it, simmer it covered in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup of rice for about 18 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the brown rice, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of rice for about 40 minutes, rest it 10 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the quinoa, rinse it, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of quinoa for about 15 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook it in a hot skillet for 3 to 5 minutes, stirring, until tender, then weigh it cooked.\n2. Prep your protein. If it is the split peas, rinse them and simmer them covered in 3 cups of water per cup of peas for 35 to 45 minutes, until tender, then drain them. If it is the lentils, rinse them well in a sieve (canned lentils only need draining and rinsing).\n3. Chop the onion fine and dice the tomato. Heat a little oil in a pot over medium, add the onion and cook 8 minutes, stirring often, until soft and golden, because a well-browned onion gives this stew its body.\n4. Stir in the garlic, a pinch of ground ginger and the harissa and cook 1 minute, until fragrant.\n5. Add the tomato and cook 3 to 4 minutes, until it breaks down into the sauce.\n6. Stir in a cup of water, a good pinch of salt and your protein, then simmer gently 12 to 15 minutes (20 to 25 for dry lentils, with a splash more water if it gets thick), stirring now and then, until very soft and thick.\n7. Prep your vegetables. If it is the collard greens, chop them and stir them in for the last 10 minutes, until tender. If it is the kale, chop it and stir it in for the last 6 to 8 minutes, until tender. If it is the tomato, dice it and stir it in for the last 3 minutes.\n8. Spoon your grain onto a plate and ladle the stew over it.",
