  "season": "garlic, ground cumin, sweet paprika, fresh cilantro and parsley, lemon, a little harissa, salt",
  "note": "Chermoula is a classic Moroccan herb-and-spice sauce; finish with a squeeze of lemon for brightness if available.",
  "method": "1. Start your grain. If it is the couscous, pour an equal amount of boiling water over it with a pinch of salt, cover and let it stand 5 minutes, fluff it with a fork, then weigh it cooked. If it is the bulgur, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of bulgur for about 12 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the quinoa, rinse it, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of quinoa for about 15 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked.\n2. Make the chermoula: stir half of the olive oil with the minced garlic, 1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp paprika, the harissa, a squeeze of lemon, a pinch of salt and a handful of chopped cilantro.\n3. Prep your protein. If it is the canned mackerel, drain and rinse it. If it is the canned salmon, drain and rinse it. Pat your protein dry and rub it with half of the chermoula, then leave it 10 minutes, so the flavor sinks in.\n4. Slice the olives, drain and rinse the chickpeas and dice your vegetables. Heat the rest of the olive oil in a nonstick pan over medium and cook all three 3 to 4 minutes, until the vegetables soften into a light sauce, then stir in the rest of the chermoula and scoop it into a bowl.\n5. If your protein is the sockeye salmon, sear it in the same pan over medium 4 to 5 minutes a side, until it flakes (145F). If your protein is the wild Atlantic salmon, sear it in the same pan over medium 4 to 5 minutes a side, until it flakes (145F). If your protein is the rainbow trout, sear it in the same pan over medium 3 to 4 minutes a side, until it flakes (145F).\n6. Spoon your grain onto a plate, top with the vegetable and chickpea mixture and set your protein on top.",
