  "season": "allspice, dried thyme, ground ginger, cinnamon, nutmeg, garlic, scallion, scotch bonnet or cayenne, salt, black pepper",
  "note": "Sriracha plus allspice and thyme builds the jerk flavor; traditional rice and peas uses coconut milk, so a tablespoon of light coconut would deepen it if you have it.",
  "method": "1. Start your grain. If it is the white rice, rinse it, simmer it covered in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup of rice for about 18 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the brown rice, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of rice for about 40 minutes, rest it 10 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the quinoa, rinse it, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of quinoa for about 15 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook it in a hot skillet for 3 to 5 minutes, stirring, until tender, then weigh it cooked.\n2. Make the jerk rub: stir the hot sauce with 1 tsp dried thyme, 1/2 tsp ground ginger, 1/2 tsp cinnamon, 1/4 tsp nutmeg, 1 tsp salt, 1/2 tsp pepper, the grated garlic and half of the onion, grated.\n3. Cut your protein into thick strips, off the bone if it has one, pat it dry and coat it with the rub. Leave it 15 minutes, so the flavor sinks in.\n4. Chop the rest of the onion and cook it in a little oil in a small pot over medium 3 minutes, until soft. Add the drained, rinsed kidney beans with a pinch of thyme and salt and warm them 3 minutes.\n5. Heat a grill pan or skillet over medium-high and cook your protein 5 to 7 minutes a side, until charred at the edges and cooked through (165F). Move it to a plate.\n6. Slice your vegetables into thin strips and char them in the same pan 3 minutes.\n7. Stir the beans into your grain, plate it with the vegetables and set your protein on top.",
