  "season": "salt, black pepper, ground cumin, dried oregano, garlic",
  "note": "Lomo saltado is Peru's most famous chifa dish, where Chinese stir-fry meets Andean ingredients. The tamari (gluten-free soy sauce) is traditional, not a shortcut. Keep the heat high and the cooking fast so the beef sears and the onion stays crunchy.",
  "method": "1. Start your grain. If it is the white rice, rinse it, simmer it covered in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup of rice for about 18 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the brown rice, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of rice for about 40 minutes, rest it 10 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the quinoa, rinse it, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of quinoa for about 15 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook it in a hot skillet for 3 to 5 minutes, stirring, until tender, then weigh it cooked.\n2. Pat your protein dry and slice it into thin strips about the width of your finger. Toss it with salt, pepper, 1/2 tsp cumin and a pinch of oregano.\n3. Cut your vegetables into thick strips or wedges and mince 2 cloves of garlic.\n4. Heat a large pan or wok over high until very hot, then add the avocado oil.\n5. Add your protein in one layer and let it sear without stirring 1 minute, then stir and cook 1 more minute, until browned and cooked through (145F). Move it to a plate.\n6. Add your vegetables to the same hot pan and stir-fry 2 to 3 minutes, so they stay a little crisp, adding the garlic for the last 30 seconds.\n7. Return your protein to the pan, splash in the soy sauce and toss 30 seconds.\n8. Spoon your grain onto a plate, pile the saltado on top and drizzle the pan juices over the grain, so it soaks them up.",
