  "season": "salt, black pepper, ground cumin, smoked paprika, garlic, dried oregano, lime",
  "note": "Pollo a la brasa is Peru's beloved roast chicken. The cumin, paprika, and garlic rub plus the fresh lime-soaked salsa criolla on top are what make it taste authentic. A little hot sauce stands in for the classic aji drizzle.",
  "method": "1. Start your grain. If it is the white rice, rinse it, simmer it covered in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup of rice for about 18 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the brown rice, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of rice for about 40 minutes, rest it 10 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the quinoa, rinse it, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of quinoa for about 15 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook it in a hot skillet for 3 to 5 minutes, stirring, until tender, then weigh it cooked.\n2. Heat the oven to 425F. Stir the olive oil, 3 cloves minced garlic, 1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp smoked paprika, 1 tsp oregano, 1/2 tsp salt and 1/2 tsp pepper into a thick rub.\n3. Pat your protein dry and coat it all over with the rub, so it browns and the flavor goes deep.\n4. Set your protein on a foil-lined tray and roast it. If it is the chicken breast, roast it 20 to 25 minutes, until deep golden and 165F inside. If it is the chicken thigh, roast it 25 to 30 minutes, until deep golden and 165F inside. If it is the chicken leg quarter, roast it 40 to 45 minutes, until deep golden and 165F inside.\n5. Meanwhile, slice your vegetables as thin as you can into a bowl. If it is the onion, rinse the slices under cold water, so the bite softens.\n6. Toss your vegetables with the salsa, a squeeze of lime, a pinch of salt and a little black pepper, then let the salsa criolla sit, so the flavors blend.\n7. Spoon your grain onto a plate, set your protein beside it and pile the salsa criolla on top. Drizzle with the hot sauce.",
