  "season": "salt, black pepper, ground cumin, chili powder, smoked paprika, garlic, dried oregano",
  "note": "This is Tex-Mex chili con carne, thick and spice-forward, ladled over rice and finished with melty cheddar. The pinto beans and double dose of cumin and chili powder give it that bowl-of-red comfort. Let the cheese melt before digging in.",
  "method": "1. Start your grain. If it is the white rice, rinse it, simmer it covered in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup of rice for about 18 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the brown rice, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of rice for about 40 minutes, rest it 10 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the quinoa, rinse it, simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of quinoa for about 15 minutes, rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then weigh it cooked. If it is the riced cauliflower, cook it in a hot skillet for 3 to 5 minutes, stirring, until tender, then weigh it cooked.\n2. Chop your vegetables fine.\n3. Heat a little oil in a deep pan over medium-high and add your protein, breaking it apart with a spoon. If it is the ground beef, brown it 5 to 6 minutes, until no pink remains (160F). If it is the ground turkey, brown it 5 to 6 minutes, until no pink remains (165F).\n4. Push the meat to one side, add your vegetables and cook 3 minutes, until soft.\n5. Stir in 2 cloves minced garlic, 1 tbsp chili powder, 1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp smoked paprika and 1 tsp oregano and cook 1 minute, until toasty, so the spices bloom in the fat.\n6. Drain and rinse the pinto beans and stir them in with a splash of water. Simmer 10 minutes, stirring now and then, until it thickens into a hearty chili, then season with salt and pepper.\n7. Spoon your grain into a bowl and ladle the chili over it. Top with the salsa and your cheese and let the cheese melt a minute before eating.",
