 "season": "garlic powder, onion powder, gluten-free Worcestershire or tamari (gluten-free soy sauce), dried thyme, salt, black pepper, beef or chicken broth",
 "note": "Old-school diner comfort food rebuilt for fat loss. Lean ground turkey and a butter-light onion gravy carry the savory hit, and the potato is mashed with skim milk so it stays creamy without the heavy cream.",
 "method": "1. Peel and cube the potato, cover it with cold salted water, bring to a boil and simmer 12 to 15 minutes, until a fork slides in easily.\n2. While it simmers, gently mix your protein with 1/2 tsp garlic powder, 1/2 tsp onion powder, 1 tsp Worcestershire sauce and a pinch of salt and pepper, and shape 1 or 2 oval patties 3/4 inch thick.\n3. Heat a little oil in a skillet over medium-high and sear the patties 3 to 4 minutes a side, until browned. Move them to a plate, because they finish cooking in the gravy.\n4. Drain the potato and mash it with a pinch of salt and pepper, the milk and half of your fat until smooth.\n5. In the same skillet, heat the rest of your fat over medium and cook 1 sliced onion 5 minutes, until soft and golden. Stir in 1/2 tsp dried thyme, 1 tsp Worcestershire sauce and the Dijon, then add a splash of broth and simmer 2 to 3 minutes into a glossy gravy, scraping up the browned bits, because that is where the flavor lives.\n6. Slide the patties back into the gravy and cover. If your protein is the ground turkey, simmer 5 to 6 minutes, until the center reads 165F. If it is the ground beef, simmer 5 to 6 minutes, until the center reads 160F.\n7. Meanwhile, steam the green beans with a splash of water, covered, 4 to 5 minutes, until bright and tender.\n8. Mound the mash on a plate, top with the patties and the onion gravy, and add the green beans on the side.",
 "oil": "Avocado oil"
