  "season": "Optional: Cajun seasoning, smoked paprika, garlic, onion, thyme, cayenne, and black pepper. Bell pepper and onion cooked in (the holy trinity) make it taste authentically Louisiana; fresh parsley to finish.",
  "note": "Very high protein, very low fat. Shrimp is one of the leanest proteins available and the riced cauliflower base keeps carbs and calories down while letting you eat a big, filling bowl.",
  "method": "1. Cook your carb first. If your carb is the sweet potato, cube it, toss with a little oil and a pinch of salt and roast at 425F for 20 to 25 minutes, turning once, until a fork slides in easily. If it is the quinoa, rinse it and simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of quinoa, 12 to 15 minutes, rest it 5 minutes, fluff it and weigh it cooked. If it is the riced cauliflower, microwave it covered with a splash of water 3 to 4 minutes, until tender, then drain off the extra water and weigh it cooked.\n2. Pat the shrimp dry, because a dry surface holds the spices and sears instead of steaming. Toss them with 1 tsp smoked paprika, 1/2 tsp garlic powder, 1/2 tsp onion powder, 1/2 tsp dried thyme, 1/4 tsp cayenne, 1/2 tsp black pepper and a pinch of salt.\n3. Heat a little oil in a large pan over medium-high. Chop your vegetables, add them and cook 4 to 5 minutes, until softened and lightly charred, then move them to a plate.\n4. Add the shrimp in one layer and cook 2 to 3 minutes a side, until pink and opaque (145F).\n5. Return the vegetables to the pan and toss 30 seconds to bring everything back to temperature.\n6. Spoon the shrimp and vegetables over your carb and top with the pico de gallo and the hot sauce. Finish with 1 tbsp chopped parsley and a squeeze of lime.",
