  "produce": "Tomato raw|Onion raw|Spinach raw|Bell pepper red raw",
  "season": "Optional: garam masala, ground cumin, coriander, turmeric, fresh ginger and garlic, and a pinch of amchur (dried mango) or a squeeze of lemon at the end. Finish with chopped cilantro.",
  "note": "A hearty, tasty Indian chana with chickpeas and sweet potato in a warm spiced tomato sauce. Carb Balance tortilla stands in for naan, and there is no rice in sight.",
  "method": "1. Heat a little oil in a deep pan over medium and cook 1/2 a diced onion 4 to 5 minutes, until soft, so it sweetens the sauce.\n2. Stir in 2 minced garlic cloves, 1 tsp grated ginger, 1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp coriander, 1/2 tsp turmeric, 1 tsp garam masala and a pinch of cayenne. Cook 30 seconds, until fragrant, because spices bloom in hot oil.\n3. Add 1 tbsp tomato paste and your vegetables, chopped, and cook 3 to 4 minutes, until they break down into a thick sauce.\n4. Add the chickpeas, drained and rinsed, with 1/2 cup of water. If your carb is the sweet potato, peel it, dice it small and add it too, so it simmers until a fork slides in easily. Cover and simmer 12 to 15 minutes, stirring now and then, until the sauce is thick. If it is the quinoa, rinse it and simmer it in a separate pot, covered, in 2 cups of water per cup of quinoa, 12 to 15 minutes, rest it 5 minutes, fluff it and weigh it cooked.\n5. Stir in a squeeze of lemon, a pinch of salt and 1 tbsp chopped cilantro.\n6. Ladle the chana masala into a bowl. If it is the quinoa, serve the chana over it. If it is the tortilla, warm it in a dry pan 20 to 30 seconds a side, so it bends without cracking, and serve it alongside for scooping.",
