  "season": "Optional: doenjang (fermented soybean paste, the star), garlic, a little gluten-free gochugaru or gochujang for heat, ginger, and sliced scallion. A few drops of toasted sesame oil off the heat.",
  "note": "Soybeans show up twice, as whole cooked soybeans for chew and as the fermented soybean paste that gives the broth its deep umami, plus tofu for silkiness. Soybeans are by far the highest-protein legume in the DB, making this the most protein-dense fully-plant dish here. The brothy, spicy, savory base is intensely satisfying for the calories, with zucchini and zucchini adding volume and baked sweet potato rounding it out.",
  "method": "1. Prick the sweet potato all over with a fork and bake it at 425F for 45 to 55 minutes, until a knife slides in with no resistance, or microwave it 6 to 8 minutes, turning once.\n2. Heat the olive oil in a pot over medium heat and cook the diced onion 3 minutes, until soft, then stir in 2 cloves of minced garlic and 1/2 tsp grated ginger for 30 seconds.\n3. Pour in 1 1/2 cups of water, then whisk in the miso paste, which stands in for doenjang (a gluten-free one if you avoid gluten), until smooth, so there are no clumps, and add a pinch of chili flakes. Bring it to a gentle simmer.\n4. Drain and rinse the soybeans, then add them to the pot with the sliced zucchini and simmer 8 minutes, until the zucchini is tender.\n5. Cube the tofu, add it and simmer 4 more minutes, until hot through and the broth tastes deep and savory. Adding it last keeps the cubes whole.\n6. Ladle the stew into a bowl, top with sliced scallion, and serve with the baked sweet potato, split open, on the side.",
