 "method": "1. Heat the oven to 425F, then press the tofu: wrap it in a clean towel and set a heavy pan on top for 10 to 15 minutes, because dry tofu crisps instead of steaming.\n2. Cube the tofu, toss it with a little oil, 1 tsp soy sauce, 1/2 tsp smoked paprika, 1/2 tsp garlic powder and a pinch of pepper, then spread it on a sheet pan and roast 25 to 30 minutes, flipping once, until golden and crisp at the edges.\n3. If it is the potato, cut it into 1-inch cubes, toss with a little oil and salt, and roast on a second sheet pan beside the tofu 25 to 30 minutes, until golden and a fork slides in.\n4. If it is the sweet potato, cut it into 1-inch cubes, toss with a little oil and salt, and roast on a second sheet pan beside the tofu 20 to 25 minutes, until the edges caramelize and a fork slides in.\n5. If it is the quinoa, rinse it and simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup of quinoa, 12 to 15 minutes, until the water is gone, then rest it 5 minutes off the heat and weigh it cooked.\n6. If it is the green beans, snap off the stem ends and steam them 4 to 5 minutes, until just tender. If it is the broccoli, cut it into small florets and steam it 4 to 5 minutes, until bright green and just tender. If it is the asparagus, snap off the woody ends and steam it 3 to 4 minutes, until tender-crisp.\n7. Simmer the edamame in a small pot of water 3 to 5 minutes, until bright green and tender, then drain it.\n8. Plate the tofu with the edamame, your carb and your vegetables, then squeeze lemon over everything, because the acid keeps roasted tofu and potatoes from tasting flat.",
