 "method": "Pile quinoa in a bowl. Top with one portion of the chicken, black beans, and corn. Add the diced pepper and shredded romaine. Spoon salsa or pico over the top and add the sliced avocado just before eating.",
 "batchQ": 2, "prepMethod": "1. Equipment: a large sheet pan, parchment, a medium pot with a lid, a fine strainer, a cutting board, a knife, a meat thermometer, paper towels and 5 containers. Heat the oven to 425F.\n2. Rinse the quinoa in the strainer under cold water for 30 seconds, because the bitter coating on the seed washes off.\n3. Simmer the quinoa covered in 2 cups of water per cup with a pinch of salt, 15 to 18 minutes, until the water is gone and each seed shows a little curl. Rest it 5 minutes off the heat, then fluff it. Weigh it cooked.\n4. While the quinoa cooks, pat the chicken breast dry with paper towels and cut it into 1-inch pieces, because dry chicken browns and wet chicken steams.\n5. Toss the chicken on the lined sheet pan with a little oil, 1 tsp salt, 1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp garlic powder, 1/2 tsp paprika and the juice of 1 lime, and spread it in one layer so the pieces do not touch.\n6. Roast the chicken 18 to 22 minutes, until the edges brown and the thickest piece reads 165F. Rest it 5 minutes so the juices settle back into the meat.\n7. Drain and rinse the black beans and the corn, no cooking needed. Cut your vegetables into bite-size pieces. If your vegetables are the romaine, shred it and keep it in its own bag so it stays crisp.\n8. Toss the cooled quinoa with the juice of 1 more lime, a small handful of chopped cilantro and a pinch of salt, so every bite tastes bright.\n9. Divide the quinoa between the 5 containers, then top each with a fifth of the chicken, the black beans, the corn and your vegetables.\n10. Keep your fat separate and add it when you eat, because cut avocado browns overnight and cheese goes rubbery when it reheats.\n11. Cool the containers with the lids off until they stop steaming, about 20 minutes, then seal them, write the date on each and get them in the fridge within 1 hour, because cooked grains spoil faster than most foods.\n12. Storage: fridge 4 days, freezer 2 months without your fat or any raw greens.\n13. Reheat: microwave 2 to 3 minutes, stirring halfway, with a splash of water if the quinoa looks dry. Top with your fat and the salsa after heating. If your vegetables are the romaine, pile it on cold at the end.",
