  "batchQ": 2, "prepMethod": "1. Equipment: 5 wide-mouth quart jars with lids as your 5 containers, a small pot with a lid, a fine strainer, a cutting board, a knife and a small jar for the dressing.\n2. Rinse the quinoa in the strainer, then simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup with a pinch of salt, 15 to 18 minutes, until the water is absorbed. Fluff it and spread it on a plate to cool, because warm quinoa steams the greens above it and goes gummy. Weigh it cooked.\n3. Prepare your protein. If it is the chickpeas, drain and rinse them, no cooking needed, and pat them dry so the dressing clings. If it is the feta, crumble it into small pieces.\n4. Make the dressing in the small jar: 4 tbsp lemon juice, 2 tbsp olive oil, 1 tsp mustard, 1 minced garlic clove, 1 tsp dried oregano, 1/2 tsp salt and black pepper, shaken hard for 20 seconds. It should taste sharp, because the quinoa and vegetables are mild.\n5. Cut your vegetables into bite-size pieces and pat them dry, because wet pieces water down the dressing.\n6. Pour 2 to 3 tbsp of dressing into the bottom of each jar.\n7. Layer bottom to top: your protein, then the cooled quinoa, then your vegetables, then the olives and your fat, so each layer shields the one above it from the dressing. If your vegetables are the romaine, pack it on top above everything so it stays dry and crisp.\n8. Seal the lids tightly and wipe the rims.\n9. Everything went in cold, so refrigerate the jars upright straight away, within 1 hour of building them, because cooked grains spoil faster than most foods.\n10. Storage: fridge 4 days. Do not freeze, because raw vegetables turn to mush when they thaw.\n11. To eat: cold. Shake the sealed jar hard for 10 seconds so the dressing coats everything, then eat from the jar or tip it into a bowl. No reheating.",
