  "method": "Microwave 3 pancakes 30 to 40 seconds, or toast them for crisp edges. Top with peanut butter, berries, and a drizzle of maple syrup.",
  "batchQ": 2, "prepMethod": "1. Equipment: a blender, a nonstick skillet or griddle, a spatula, a cooling rack, parchment and 5 containers.\n2. Blend the oats on their own for 20 seconds first, until they turn to a rough flour, because whole oats make a gritty pancake.\n3. Add your protein, 1 tsp baking powder, 1 tsp vanilla, 1 tsp cinnamon, a pinch of salt and about 1/2 cup water, and blend 30 seconds until smooth and pourable. If your fruit is the banana, blend it in too, because it sweetens and binds the batter. If your fruit is the blueberries, keep them whole for the top.\n4. Let the batter rest 3 minutes, so the oats soak up the water and the baking powder starts to bubble, which is what makes the pancakes fluffy.\n5. Heat the skillet over medium and give it a light mist of cooking spray, because medium heat cooks the inside before the outside scorches.\n6. Pour scant 1/4-cup pools of batter and cook 2 to 3 minutes, until bubbles form across the top and the edges look dry, then flip and cook 1 to 2 minutes more, until golden and the center springs back (160F inside), so the batter is set through.\n7. Move each pancake to the rack in a single layer, because stacking them hot traps steam and makes them soggy.\n8. Cool the pancakes completely on the rack, about 15 minutes.\n9. Divide the pancakes between the 5 containers with a parchment square between the layers so they do not stick, then seal, write the date on each and refrigerate.\n10. Storage: fridge 4 days, freezer 2 months laid flat in a zip bag with parchment between them.\n11. Reheat: microwave one portion 30 to 40 seconds, or toast them for crisp edges, or warm them from frozen at 350F in the oven for 5 minutes.\n12. Top with the peanut butter, your fruit and a drizzle of the maple syrup after heating.",
