  "method": "Grab a jar and eat cold. Stir before eating and top with fresh berries and chopped almonds.",
  "batchQ": 3, "prepMethod": "1. Equipment: a large bowl, a whisk, 5 half-pint jars with lids as your 5 containers, and 5 small bags for the almonds.\n2. Whisk your protein in the bowl with 2 1/2 cups unsweetened almond milk, 2 tsp vanilla, 1/2 tsp cinnamon, a tiny pinch of salt and the maple syrup until there are no clumps, because clumps never dissolve once the mix thickens.\n3. Stir in the oats so they soften overnight and give the pudding body.\n4. If your fat is the chia seeds, whisk them in now, wait 5 minutes, then whisk again hard, because the seeds start to gel in clumps and the second whisk is what makes a smooth pudding.\n5. Divide the mix between the 5 jars, filling each about 2/3 full, and seal the lids.\n6. Everything is cool already, so refrigerate the jars straight away, at least 4 hours or overnight, until thick and spoonable.\n7. Shake each jar once after the first 30 minutes, so nothing sinks into a layer at the bottom.\n8. Chop the almonds and pack them into the 5 small bags, one per jar, so they stay crunchy.\n9. Keep your fruit out of the jars until you eat, because cut fruit weeps juice into the pudding by day two.\n10. Storage: fridge 5 days. Do not freeze, because the pudding turns watery when it thaws.\n11. To eat: cold. Stir the jar, then top it with your fruit and the almonds.",
