  "method": "Microwave 2 cups 40 to 50 seconds, or eat cold. Top with a little Greek yogurt or maple syrup.",
  "batchQ": 2, "prepMethod": "1. Equipment: a 12-cup muffin tin, 12 silicone liners or nonstick spray, 2 bowls, a whisk and 4 containers. Heat the oven to 350F.\n2. Line or grease all 12 cups, into the corners, so the cups release clean.\n3. Stir the oats, 1 1/2 tsp baking powder, 2 tsp pumpkin pie spice, 1 tsp cinnamon and 1/4 tsp salt together in one bowl, so the baking powder and spice spread evenly and every cup rises and tastes like pie.\n4. Whisk your protein in the second bowl with the maple syrup, 1 tsp vanilla and 1/2 cup unsweetened almond milk until smooth. If your produce is the canned pumpkin, whisk it in, because it keeps the cups moist for days. If your produce is the banana, mash it and whisk it in, because it keeps the cups moist and sweet.\n5. Pour the wet mix into the dry and stir until just moistened, then fold in the pecans, chopped.\n6. Spoon the batter evenly into the 12 cups, filling each nearly to the top, and press a few extra pecan pieces on top.\n7. Bake 24 to 28 minutes, until the tops are firm, spring back when pressed, a toothpick comes out clean and the center reads 160F.\n8. Cool the tin 10 minutes, because the cups firm up as they rest and then lift out whole.\n9. Pack 3 cups into each of the 4 containers, lids off until they stop steaming, then seal, write the date on each and refrigerate within 2 hours.\n10. Storage: fridge 4 days, freezer 2 months.\n11. Reheat: microwave 3 cups 40 to 50 seconds from the fridge, or about 75 seconds from frozen, or eat them cold. A drizzle of the maple syrup on top.",
