 "method": "1. Whisk the protein powder into the almond milk until smooth. 2. Whisk in the chia seeds. 3. Wait 5 minutes and whisk again hard. 4. Chill at least 3 hours. 5. Top with berries.",
 "batchQ": 2, "prepMethod": "1. Equipment: a bowl, a whisk, 5 jars with lids, and 5 small cups or bags for the berries.\n2. Whisk the protein powder into the almond milk in the bowl with a pinch of salt and 1/2 tsp cinnamon until there are no lumps; the powder goes in before the chia because it blends smooth into thin liquid and clumps in thick.\n3. Whisk in the chia seeds.\n4. Rest the bowl 5 minutes, then whisk again hard, which breaks up the clumps that form as the seeds start to swell and is what stops the pudding setting in lumps.\n5. Pour the mix evenly into the 5 jars and screw on the lids.\n6. Wash your fruit and pat dry. If your fruit is the strawberries, hull them and slice them into quarters so they fit on a spoon. Pack the berries in the small cups, one per jar, because berries stirred in now go soft and bleed into the pudding by morning.\n7. Chill the jars at least 3 hours, or overnight, so the chia swells and sets the pudding; it is already cool, so the jars go straight into the fridge.\n8. Storage: fridge 5 days, with the fruit in its own cup. Do not freeze, because the pudding turns watery when it thaws.\n9. To eat: cold, no reheating. Stir the pudding, tip the berries on top, and add a little lemon zest if you want it brighter; the pinch of salt matters more than any sweetener here.",
