 "method": "1. Soften the onion, pepper and garlic in the oil for 8 minutes. 2. Add the beans, tomato and a little water and simmer 15 minutes. 3. Blend about a third of it so it is thick, then stir it back in. 4. Serve over rice.",
 "batchQ": 3, "prepMethod": "1. Equipment: a large pot with a lid, a small pot for the grain, a stick blender, a colander, a cutting board, a knife, a ladle and 5 containers.\n2. Cook your grain in the small pot: rinse it, then simmer it covered in salted water until tender and the water is absorbed (white rice takes about 18 minutes in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup; brown rice 40 to 45 minutes in 2 cups per cup). Rest it 5 minutes off the heat and fluff it. Weigh it cooked.\n3. Dice the onion and mince the garlic.\n4. Warm the olive oil in the large pot over medium, add the onion and the garlic, and cook 6 to 8 minutes, stirring, until soft and sweet. If it is the bell pepper, dice it and cook it with the onion so it softens.\n5. Stir in 2 tsp ground cumin and 1 tsp dried oregano and cook 30 seconds, until fragrant, so the spices bloom in the oil.\n6. Drain and rinse the black beans in the colander, then add them with 1 bay leaf, 1/2 tsp salt and 3 cups of water. If it is the tomato, chop the tomato and add it now so it breaks down into the soup. Bring it to a simmer and cook, lid on a crack, 15 to 20 minutes, so the beans soften further and the flavors come together.\n7. Remove the bay leaf, then blend about a third of the soup with the stick blender, so it turns thick and creamy while most of the beans stay whole.\n8. Stir in 1 tbsp vinegar, taste, and add salt if it is flat. The sharp finish is the classic Cuban touch.\n9. Divide the cooked grain between the 5 containers and ladle the soup over it straight away: a full pot cools too slowly.\n10. Cool the containers with the lids off until they stop steaming, then seal, write the date on each, and refrigerate within 1 hour, because cooked rice spoils fastest.\n11. Keep the salsa and the lime out of the containers, so the salsa stays fresh and the lime keeps its brightness.\n12. Storage: fridge 4 days, freezer 3 months.\n13. Reheat: microwave 2 to 3 minutes, stirring halfway, or warm it on the stove with a splash of water, because the grain soaks up the liquid overnight. Top the bowl with the salsa, a big squeeze of lime, cilantro and a little raw onion.",
 "makes": 5,
