 "batchQ": 3, "prepMethod": "1. Equipment: a large pot with a lid, a small pot for the grain, a frying pan, a cutting board, a knife, a ladle, a meat thermometer and 5 containers.\n2. Cook your protein first so it can rest while you chop: pat it dry, season it with salt and pepper, and cook it in the hot frying pan with a little of the olive oil over medium-high, 5 to 7 minutes a side, until it reads 165F in the thickest part. Rest it 5 minutes so the juices settle, then shred it with two forks.\n3. Cook your carb: if it is the brown rice, rinse the brown rice and simmer it covered in the small pot in 2 cups of salted water per cup of rice, 40 to 45 minutes, until tender, then fluff it and weigh it cooked; if it is the potato, peel the potato and cut it into 3/4-inch cubes.\n4. Dice the onion. Warm the rest of the olive oil in the large pot over medium, add the onion, and cook 5 to 6 minutes, until soft. If it is the carrots, cut them into 1/2-inch coins and cook them with the onion so they soften. If it is the celery, slice it and cook it with the onion.\n5. Add the broth, 2 cups of water, 1 tsp dried thyme, 1 bay leaf and 1/2 tsp salt, and bring it to a simmer. If it is the potato, add the cubes now and simmer 15 minutes, until a knife slides in easily.\n6. Simmer, lid on a crack, 15 minutes, until the vegetables are tender. If it is the green beans, trim them, cut them into 1-inch pieces and add them for the last 5 minutes so they stay green and snappy.\n7. Stir in the shredded meat and simmer 2 minutes to heat it through. If it is the brown rice, stir the cooked rice in now too.\n8. Remove the bay leaf, squeeze in the juice of half a lemon, and taste for salt. The lemon is what stops it tasting flat.\n9. Ladle the soup into the 5 containers straight away, because a full pot cools too slowly.\n10. Cool the containers with the lids off until they stop steaming, then seal, write the date on each, and refrigerate within 1 hour, because cooked starches spoil fastest.\n11. Storage: fridge 4 days, freezer 3 months.\n12. Reheat: microwave 2 to 3 minutes, stirring halfway, or warm it on the stove, with a splash of water if the starch has soaked up the broth. Finish with parsley and black pepper.",
