 "batchQ": 3, "prepMethod": "1. Equipment: a heavy pot with a lid, a small pot, a frying pan, a cutting board, a knife, a ladle, a meat thermometer and 5 containers.\n2. Cook your protein first so it can rest while the stew simmers: pat it dry, season it with salt and 1 tsp Cajun seasoning, and cook it in the hot frying pan with a little of your fat over medium-high, 5 to 7 minutes a side, until it reads 165F in the thickest part. Rest it 5 minutes so the juices settle, then cut it into bite-size pieces.\n3. Cook your grain in the small pot: rinse it, then simmer it covered in salted water until tender and the water is absorbed (white rice takes about 18 minutes in 1 1/2 cups of water per cup; brown rice 40 to 45 minutes in 2 cups per cup). Rest it 5 minutes off the heat and fluff it. Weigh it cooked.\n4. Dice the onion and chop the tomato.\n5. Warm the rest of your fat in the heavy pot over medium, add the onion, and cook 8 minutes, stirring, until soft and sweet, because this slow start is where the flavor comes from. If it is the bell pepper, dice it and cook it with the onion. If it is the celery, slice it and cook it with the onion.\n6. Stir in 1 tbsp Cajun seasoning, 1 tsp dried thyme and a pinch of cayenne and cook 1 minute, until fragrant, so the spices bloom in the fat instead of tasting raw.\n7. Add the tomato, the broth, 1 cup of water, 1 bay leaf and 1/2 tsp salt and bring it to a simmer. If it is the okra, slice it into 1/2-inch rounds and add it now, because 20 minutes of simmering cooks away its slime and lets it thicken the stew.\n8. Simmer, lid off, 20 minutes, stirring now and then, until the stew has thickened. Stir in the meat, remove the bay leaf, and taste for salt.\n9. Divide the cooked grain between the 5 containers and ladle the stew over it straight away: a full pot cools too slowly.\n10. Cool the containers with the lids off until they stop steaming, then seal, write the date on each, and refrigerate within 1 hour, because cooked rice spoils fastest.\n11. Storage: fridge 4 days, freezer 3 months.\n12. Reheat: microwave 2 to 3 minutes, stirring halfway, or warm it on the stove, with a splash of water if the grain has soaked up the stew. Hot sauce at the table.",
