 "batchQ": 3, "prepMethod": "1. Equipment: a large pot with a lid, a stick blender (or a regular blender), a colander, a cutting board, a knife, a ladle and 5 containers.\n2. Dice the onion and mince the garlic.\n3. Warm the olive oil in the pot over medium, add the onion and the garlic, and cook 6 minutes, stirring, until soft and sweet. If it is the carrots, dice them into 1/2-inch pieces and cook them with the onion from the start so they soften.\n4. Stir in 1 tsp dried sage, a pinch of nutmeg and plenty of black pepper and cook 30 seconds, until fragrant.\n5. Drain and rinse your beans in the colander, because the liquid in the can makes the soup cloudy and salty.\n6. Add the beans, the canned pumpkin, 4 cups of water and 1/2 tsp salt, stir well so the pumpkin dissolves into the liquid, and bring it to a simmer.\n7. Simmer, lid on a crack, 12 to 15 minutes, stirring now and then so the pumpkin does not catch on the bottom, until everything is tender.\n8. Blend about half of it with the stick blender (or ladle half into a blender and pour it back) so it is creamy but still has whole beans in it. If it is the kale, strip the leaves off the stems, tear them into pieces, and simmer them in the soup 5 minutes after blending so they soften but keep their color.\n9. Stir in 1 tbsp vinegar and taste for salt. The sharp note is what keeps a sweet soup from tasting like dessert.\n10. Ladle the soup evenly into the 5 containers straight away: a full pot cools too slowly and sits for hours in the 40 to 140F range where bacteria grow fastest.\n11. Cool the containers with the lids off until they stop steaming, about 20 to 30 minutes, then seal them, write the date on each, and get them in the fridge within 2 hours of cooking.\n12. Storage: fridge 4 days, freezer 3 months.\n13. Reheat: microwave 2 to 3 minutes, stirring halfway, or warm it on the stove, with a splash of water because it thickens as it sits. Finish with black pepper.",
