 "method": "1. Soften the onion, carrot, celery and garlic in the oil. 2. Add the tomato, potato, lentils and water and simmer 20 minutes. 3. Stir in the kale.",
 "batchQ": 3, "prepMethod": "1. Equipment: a large pot with a lid (6 to 8 quarts), a sieve, a cutting board, a knife, a vegetable peeler, a wooden spoon, a ladle and 5 containers.\n2. Dice the onion, mince the garlic and chop the tomato. Peel your carb and cut it into 3/4-inch cubes so the pieces cook through in the same time as the lentils.\n3. Warm the olive oil in the pot over medium, add the onion and the garlic, and cook 6 minutes, stirring, until soft and sweet. If it is the carrots, dice them into 1/2-inch pieces and cook them with the onion from the start. If it is the celery, slice it 1/2 inch thick and cook it with the onion from the start.\n4. Stir in 1 tsp dried thyme and plenty of black pepper, then add the tomato and cook 2 minutes, until it starts to break down.\n5. Rinse the lentils in the sieve and add them to the pot with your carb and 1 bay leaf, then add 1 tsp salt and water to cover everything by an inch, about 3 cups of water per cup of dry lentils. Bring it to a boil, then lower it to a simmer.\n6. Simmer, lid on a crack, 20 to 25 minutes, stirring now and then, until the lentils are tender but still hold their shape and a knife slides through the cubes with no resistance. Add a splash of water if it looks dry before then. If it is the kale, strip the leaves off the stems, tear them into pieces, and stir them in for the last 5 minutes so they soften but keep their color.\n7. Remove the bay leaf, then stir in 1 tsp Dijon mustard and 1 tbsp red wine vinegar and taste for salt. The mustard and vinegar are what make it taste French instead of flat.\n8. Ladle the stew evenly into the 5 containers straight away: a full pot cools too slowly and sits for hours in the 40 to 140F range where bacteria grow fastest.\n9. Cool the containers with the lids off until they stop steaming, about 20 to 30 minutes, then seal them, write the date on each, and get them in the fridge within 2 hours of cooking.\n10. Storage: fridge 4 days, freezer 3 months.\n11. Reheat: microwave 2 to 3 minutes, stirring halfway, or warm it on the stove, with a splash of water because the lentils keep drinking the liquid in the fridge. Finish with black pepper and parsley.",
