 "batchQ": 3, "prepMethod": "1. Equipment: a large deep skillet with a lid, cooking spray, a heatproof bowl, a cutting board, a knife, a colander and 5 containers.\n2. Put the dry TVP in the bowl with 1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp chili powder, 1/2 tsp dried oregano and 1/2 tsp salt, pour over just enough hot water to cover, and leave it 5 to 10 minutes, until it has swelled and soaked the water up. Seasoning the soaking water puts the flavour inside the crumbles.\n3. Dice the onion and cut your vegetables into 1/2-inch pieces. If it is the tomato, keep it aside to dice fresh over the top when you eat, because cooked into the sauce it just disappears.\n4. Mist the skillet with cooking spray, heat it over medium-high and cook the onion 5 to 6 minutes, stirring now and then, until soft and browned at the edges; if it is the bell pepper, cook it with the onion from the start; if it is the zucchini, cook it with the onion from the start.\n5. Add the soaked TVP and the enchilada sauce with 1/2 cup of water and simmer 5 minutes, stirring, so the crumbles take on the sauce. If your carb is the black beans, drain and rinse them and stir them in now, because canned beans are already cooked and only need to heat through.\n6. Tear the corn tortillas into 1-inch pieces and stir them in, pressing them under the sauce, then cover and simmer 4 to 5 minutes, until they have softened into the sauce but still hold their shape. That is the enchilada texture without the rolling.\n7. Taste it and add salt, a squeeze of lime and a handful of chopped cilantro.\n8. Ladle the skillet into the 5 containers straight away; a full pan takes too long to cool to be safe. Leave the lids off until the steam stops, 20 to 30 minutes, then seal, write the date on each, and refrigerate within 2 hours.\n9. Keep the avocado whole and uncut until you eat, because cut avocado browns overnight. If it is the tomato, dice it fresh over the top when you eat too.\n10. Storage: fridge 4 days, freezer 3 months without the avocado.\n11. Reheat: microwave 2 to 3 minutes, stirring halfway, or warm it in a pan with a splash of water, because the tortillas soak up liquid overnight. Top with sliced avocado, cilantro and lime.",
