 "batchQ": 3, "prepMethod": "1. Equipment: a large deep pan with a lid, a medium pot with a lid, a cutting board, a knife, paper towels, and 5 containers.\n2. Press the tofu between paper towels under a heavy pan for 15 minutes, then cut it into 1-inch cubes; dry tofu browns, wet tofu steams.\n3. Dice the onion and your vegetables into even 1/2-inch pieces, chop the apricots, and drain and rinse the chickpeas.\n4. Heat the olive oil in the pan over medium-high and brown the tofu 8 to 10 minutes, turning so every side gets color, then stir in 2 tsp ground cumin, 1 tsp paprika, 1 tsp ground ginger, 1/2 tsp cinnamon and 1 tsp salt for 30 seconds so the spices bloom in the oil.\n5. Add the onion and your vegetables and cook 5 minutes, stirring, until the onion is soft.\n6. Add the chickpeas, the apricots and 1 cup water, cover, and simmer 12 to 15 minutes, until the sauce is thick and everything is tender; the apricots half-melt, which is where the sweetness comes from.\n7. Cook your grain in the pot while the stew simmers (couscous: pour 1 1/4 cups of boiling salted water over each cup, cover, leave it 5 minutes off the heat and fluff it; quinoa: rinse it and simmer it covered in 2 cups of water per cup, 15 to 18 minutes, until the germ spirals out and the water is gone, then rest it 5 minutes and fluff). Weigh it cooked.\n8. Spread the grain on a tray to cool fast, because warm grain sealed in a container goes gummy and sits too long in the danger zone.\n9. Cool the stew with the lid off until it stops steaming, 20 to 30 minutes.\n10. Divide the grain between the 5 containers and spoon the stew over it, seal, write the date on each, and refrigerate within 1 hour of the grain coming off the heat.\n11. Keep the lemon and the herbs separate and add them when you eat, because mint and cilantro blacken overnight.\n12. Storage: fridge 4 days, freezer 3 months.\n13. Reheat: microwave 2 to 3 minutes with a splash of water, stirring halfway, until steaming hot. Finish with lemon and torn mint or cilantro.",
