 "method": "1. Poach the chicken in the broth 15 minutes, then shred it. 2. Soften the onion and garlic in a little oil 5 minutes, then add the cumin and oregano. 3. Add the salsa verde, the broth, the beans and your vegetable and simmer 15 minutes. 4. Mash a ladle of beans against the pot to thicken it and stir the chicken back in. 5. Top with the yogurt and avocado.",
 "prepMethod": "1. Equipment: a large pot with a lid, a meat thermometer, two forks, a ladle, a cutting board, a knife, and 4 shallow containers plus 4 small tubs for the yogurt.\n2. Poach your protein first: lay it in the pot, cover it with 4 cups broth and a pinch of salt, bring it to a bare simmer and cook 15 to 18 minutes, until it reads 165F in the thickest part.\n3. Lift the meat onto a board, rest it 5 minutes so the juices settle, then shred it with the two forks. Pour the broth into a bowl and keep it; it is the base of the chili.\n4. Dice the onion and your vegetables into 1/2-inch pieces so they cook evenly, and drain and rinse your beans.\n5. Coat the empty pot with a light mist of cooking spray, set it over medium, and soften the onion and 3 cloves garlic (minced) 5 minutes, then stir in 2 tsp ground cumin, 1 tsp dried oregano and a pinch of chili flakes for 30 seconds.\n6. Add the salsa verde, the poaching broth, your beans and your vegetables, and simmer 15 minutes, until everything is tender.\n7. Mash a ladle of the beans against the side of the pot and stir it back in, which thickens the chili with no cream.\n8. Stir the shredded meat back in and simmer 2 minutes to heat through; stored in the chili it stays moist instead of picking up the stale taste plain chicken gets.\n9. Ladle the chili straight into the 4 shallow containers and cool them with the lids off until they stop steaming, 20 to 30 minutes, because a full pot cools too slowly. Then seal, write the date on each, and refrigerate within 2 hours.\n10. Spoon the yogurt into the 4 small tubs and keep the lime and cilantro separate; the yogurt splits if it is reheated with the chili.\n11. If your fat is the avocado, keep it out of the containers and dice it fresh over each bowl as you eat, because cut avocado browns within hours.\n12. Storage: fridge 3 days, freezer 3 months for the chili alone; the yogurt does not freeze.\n13. Reheat: microwave covered 2 to 3 minutes, stirring halfway, until steaming hot, with a splash of water if it has thickened. Finish it with the yogurt, lime, cilantro and your fat.",
