 "method": "1. Chop the cooked shrimp. 2. Stir it with a spoon of ketchup, lime juice, hot sauce and a splash of cold water into a loose cocktail sauce. 3. Fold in the diced cucumber and the pico de gallo. 4. Eat it cold with a spoon.",
 "prepMethod": "1. Equipment: a medium pot, a bowl of ice water, a slotted spoon, a bowl, a knife, a thermometer, 4 containers (small jars with lids are ideal) and 4 small tubs for the vegetables.\n2. Cook the shrimp: if you buy them raw, poach them in simmering salted water with a bay leaf and a squeeze of lime, 2 to 3 minutes, until pink, opaque and curled into a C (145F), then lift them straight into the ice water so they stop cooking. If you buy them already cooked, thaw them in the fridge overnight and skip the pot.\n3. Drain the shrimp well, pat them dry and chop them into bite-size pieces, because wet shrimp water down the sauce.\n4. Stir 4 tbsp ketchup, the juice of 2 limes, the hot sauce, a pinch of salt and 4 tbsp cold water into a loose, pourable sauce. It should taste sharp, because the shrimp are sweet.\n5. Fold the shrimp through the sauce so every piece is coated. Sitting in the tangy sauce keeps the shrimp moist and tasting fresh.\n6. Divide the shrimp and sauce between the 4 containers.\n7. Cut your vegetables into 1/2-inch dice and pack them with the pico de gallo and the cilantro in the small tubs, one per serving, so they stay crisp and do not water down the sauce.\n8. Everything is already cool from the ice bath, so seal the containers, date them, and refrigerate straight away.\n9. Storage: fridge 3 days; cooked shrimp fades fast, so this is a cook-twice-a-week dish, and plain poached shrimp freezes 2 months if you want to stretch it. The vegetables are at their best within 2.\n10. To eat: cold, with a spoon. Tip the vegetables and pico into the container, stir, and add more hot sauce and lime to taste, and a little chopped jalapeno if you like heat. No reheating.",
 "makes": 4,
